EX={}
def add(slug,t): EX[slug]=t
add("iul-vs-term-life-plus-investing-for-high-earners","""## A practical test before you decide
Ask each side to put their proposal on paper with the same inputs: your age, health class, budget, and time horizon. For the IUL, request the guaranteed illustration, a conservative-credit illustration, and an in-force illustration at age 70 and 85. For term plus investing, write down exactly where the monthly difference will go, what it will cost in fees, and what happens when the term ends and you still need coverage. The proposal that still looks acceptable when its assumptions are stressed is usually the more honest one.

## Don't forget what happens when term ends
The big weakness of term is that it expires. If you still need coverage at the end of the term, a new policy will cost far more at an older age, and your health may limit your options. Some term policies can be converted to permanent coverage without a new exam, which can reduce that risk, so ask about conversion rights and deadlines.
""")
add("fixed-indexed-annuity-vs-cd-vs-bond-where-does-an-fia-fit","""## Interest rates change the picture
CD rates and bond yields move with market interest rates, and so do the caps and participation rates that annuity companies offer. When rates are high, CDs and bonds can look attractive and caps tend to be more generous. When rates are low, CDs pay little and annuity caps shrink. That means the right comparison is not fixed forever. It is worth re-checking current offers when you are ready to act rather than relying on what a friend was offered a few years ago.

## A note on laddering
Many savers spread money across CDs or bonds with different maturity dates, called a ladder, to keep some cash available each year. An annuity does not work that way, so if you want liquidity, consider keeping part of your money in a ladder and committing only what you will not need to an annuity.
""")
add("how-much-life-insurance-do-high-income-earners-need","""## A worked example, in words
Picture a household in which one parent earns a high salary, the other earns less, and they have two young children, a mortgage, and goals for college. The needs analysis adds the mortgage balance, a number of years of income replacement after subtracting the surviving parent's earnings, college funding for both children, and funeral and legal costs. It then subtracts savings and existing coverage. The result may be a number several times larger than a simple multiple suggests, or much smaller if the family already has large assets. The point is that the same salary can lead to very different answers.

## Coverage in layers
Some families layer policies, for example one long-term policy for the mortgage period, another for the years the children are in school, and a permanent policy for lifelong needs. Layering can lower cost because coverage steps down as obligations shrink, and it makes reviews easier.
""")
add("is-cash-value-life-insurance-a-good-retirement-supplement","""## A balanced bottom line
For most people, the sensible order is to capture employer matches, fund retirement accounts and an HSA if eligible, and build an emergency fund before considering a cash value policy for retirement purposes. For those who have done all that and also need permanent coverage, cash value life insurance can be one of several tools. It can look appealing in a sales illustration, so judge it by the guaranteed numbers, the costs, and how well it still works if credits come in lower than projected.

## Keep the policy healthy
If you do own one, review it annually. Ask the insurer for an in-force illustration, check that the cash value is tracking the projection, and adjust premiums early if it is falling behind. Small adjustments made early are far easier than rescue attempts late in life.
""")
add("life-insurance-policy-loan-does-it-avoid-taxes","""## A simple illustration with made-up numbers
Suppose someone has a policy with a cash value of a certain amount and borrows a portion each year to supplement retirement income. Each loan accrues interest, so the debt grows even when no new money is borrowed. If the debt eventually approaches the cash value, the policy may need extra premium to stay in force. If it lapses, the outstanding loan can be treated as a distribution, which may create taxable income. These numbers are hypothetical, but they show why monitoring matters as much as the original plan.

## Keep documentation
Keep copies of your loan agreements, annual statements, and any illustrations you were shown. If you later need to explain the transactions to a tax preparer, those records make the work much easier and reduce the chance of mistakes on a return.
""")
add("what-happens-to-a-fixed-indexed-annuity-when-you-die","""## Special situations to be aware of
Some contracts include enhanced death benefits or bonus features that affect what beneficiaries receive, and some have rules that reduce or forfeit certain bonuses if death occurs within a specified period. If the annuity was bought with a bonus, ask how that bonus is treated at death. Also consider contracts held in trusts or by entities, which can change who the beneficiaries are and how taxes apply. Your attorney and the insurer can confirm the details for your situation.

## Talk to your family
It is easy to assume your family knows about your annuity. Many do not. A short conversation and a one-page summary listing the insurer, contract number, beneficiaries, and a contact person can save months of searching and prevent unclaimed funds.
""")
add("how-much-does-final-expense-insurance-cost-at-65-70-75","""## Example of how to read a quote
When you receive a quote, check that it lists the age, sex, tobacco status, coverage amount, underwriting class, and whether the benefit is immediate or graded. Compare these line by line with other quotes. Two quotes can look very different simply because one assumes a graded class and another assumes immediate benefits. A lower-looking premium may be buying you less. If a quote does not list these details, ask for them in writing.

## Affordability matters more than the lowest price
The best policy is one you can keep paying. A policy that lapses after a few years pays nothing, and the premiums are lost. Choose a coverage amount that fits comfortably in your monthly budget, and set up automatic payments, ideally from an account that will not be overdrawn, so you do not miss a payment.
""")
add("graded-death-benefit-final-expense-explained","""## What to do if you already have a graded policy
If you already own a graded policy, you are not necessarily stuck. After the grading period passes, the full benefit applies. If your health has improved or you were placed in a graded class by mistake, it may be possible to apply for a new policy with immediate benefits, but do not cancel the old one until the new one is approved. Compare the cost and the new waiting periods. If you keep the graded policy, mark the date the full benefit begins on your calendar and share it with your family so they know what to expect.

## Reading the policy itself
The illustration or brochure usually describes the grading period in general terms, but the policy contract has the exact language. Ask for a sample contract before you apply and read the section on the death benefit and any limitations. It takes a few minutes and can prevent painful surprises for your family.
""")
add("can-i-get-life-insurance-at-80-with-health-problems","""## What if you are declined?
A decline from one carrier does not mean every carrier will say no. Underwriting guidelines differ, and a condition that is a problem for one company may be acceptable to another. If you receive a decline, ask the agent about guaranteed-issue options, which accept applicants regardless of health, and about whether waiting for a recovery period before reapplying could change the result. Also consider alternatives such as setting aside savings in a payable-on-death account.

## Think about the beneficiary's experience
Make sure the beneficiary knows the policy exists, has the insurer's phone number, and knows where the paperwork is. A claim can only be paid if someone files it, and unclaimed benefits are more common than people realize. A short note in your important papers can make the difference.
""")
add("final-expense-vs-burial-insurance-vs-prepaid-funeral-plan","""## Mixing approaches sensibly
Some families combine tools: a modest final expense policy for flexibility, plus a prepaid arrangement for items they feel strongly about, or simply a written record of wishes. If you combine them, avoid paying twice for the same thing. Add up what each covers, and make sure the total fits your budget and your family's likely needs.

## Check the paperwork before you pay
For any prepaid plan, ask for a written contract and read the cancellation, transfer, and refund provisions. For any insurance policy, read the benefit description, premium schedule, and exclusions. If anything is unclear, ask a family member or another trusted person to read it with you before you sign.
""")
add("how-much-does-a-funeral-cost-and-who-pays-without-a-policy","""## A conversation worth having
Many families never discuss funeral wishes until it is too late. A short, honest conversation about burial or cremation, religious or cultural traditions, and who should make decisions can prevent disagreements. Write the answers down, and keep them where the right person can find them. This costs nothing and may be the most valuable preparation of all.

## Where to get help locally
If cost is a worry, ask the funeral home about payment plans, and contact your county or city social services office about local assistance. Veterans' families can ask about burial benefits through the Department of Veterans Affairs. Religious organizations and community groups sometimes also offer support.
""")
add("should-i-buy-life-insurance-for-my-child-gerber-style-whole-life","""## How to decide in a single sitting
Sit down with your partner and ask four questions. Are both parents covered adequately? Are emergency savings and retirement contributions on track? Is there a family health history that makes future insurability a worry? Can we afford the premium for many years without strain? If the answers are yes, no, yes, yes, a child's policy may be a reasonable addition. If the first two answers are no, it can wait. This simple filter keeps the decision grounded in your actual situation instead of a sales presentation.

## Transferring ownership later
Many juvenile policies are owned by a parent during childhood and can be transferred to the child as an adult. Ask how and when ownership transfers, whether premiums change, and what taxes or paperwork are involved. Knowing the plan for adulthood helps you see whether the policy will still make sense then.
""")
add("cash-value-life-insurance-for-kids-and-college","""## A realistic expectation
Families sometimes imagine a policy that grows into a large tuition fund. In reality, the cash value of a typical child's policy after eighteen years may cover only part of the cost of college, and drawing on it reduces the lifetime coverage you hoped to keep. Think of the policy as a supplement and not the primary plan, and set your expectations using the guaranteed values, not the projected ones.

## Coordinate with your overall plan
Before buying, make sure your own coverage is adequate, you are saving enough for retirement, and you have an emergency fund. College can be financed in many ways, including scholarships, work, and loans, but retirement cannot be financed with loans. Funding college at the expense of your own security is rarely wise.
""")
add("life-insurance-vs-529-for-college-savings","""## State differences matter
Many states offer income tax deductions or credits for contributions to their own 529 plans, while others offer none, and some allow deductions for contributions to any state's plan. Because these rules vary, a good first step is to check your state's treasury or education savings program website. If your state offers a meaningful tax benefit, it may tilt the comparison toward a 529 for the education portion of your savings.

## Revisit as your child grows
Your plan does not need to be permanent. As your child approaches college age, review the balances, the cost estimates, and any scholarship opportunities. If the 529 is overfunded, you may be able to change the beneficiary, and if it is underfunded, you can adjust contributions or explore other sources. Staying flexible is part of a good plan.
""")
add("guaranteed-insurability-rider-juvenile-life-insurance","""## An example of how it plays out
Imagine a child with a policy that includes the rider. At one of the option dates, the young adult has developed a health condition that would make a new policy expensive. Because of the rider, they can still buy additional coverage at the standard rate for their age, without a medical exam, up to the stated limit. If they never develop a condition, they may choose to buy more coverage anyway, or let the option lapse. The value of the rider is highest for those who develop a condition and lowest for those who stay healthy, which is the nature of insurance.

## Keep track of the option dates
Because options expire, mark the option dates on your calendar when you buy the policy and share them with your child as they grow. A rider that goes unused because nobody remembered it is wasted money.
""")
add("protect-kids-college-plans-if-i-die-early","""## Make the plan visible
The best arrangements fail if no one knows about them. After you set up insurance, accounts, and legal documents, write a short summary that lists each item, where the documents are, and who to call. Share it with your spouse or a trusted person, and update it each year. A few minutes of organization now can spare your family confusion and delay later.

## Review every few years
Children grow, incomes change, and college cost estimates move. Revisit your life insurance amount, your savings, and your documents every few years and after major life events such as a new baby, a move, a career change, or a divorce. Small updates keep the plan matched to your real situation.
""")
